$post_body = <<<'HTML'
<p>A professional should measure a patio door for <a href="%BASE%/window-treatments/window-blinds/vertical-blinds/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">vertical blinds</a> in almost every case, because a patio door opening is wider and taller than a standard window and a small measuring error there is far more costly to fix. A homeowner with a tape measure can get a rough number for budgeting, but the final order should come from a measurement taken by someone who installs vertical blinds for a living. The gap between a close guess and an exact measurement is where most reorders and wasted money come from.</p>
%IMAGE1%
<h2 id="why-is-a-patio-door-harder-to-measure-than-a-window">Why Is a Patio Door Harder to Measure Than a Window?</h2>
<p><strong>A patio door brings a few extra variables that a standard window does not have.</strong></p>
<ul class="bp-list">
  <li>The opening is usually 6 to 9 feet wide, so even a small measuring mistake becomes a larger gap in the finished product.</li>
  <li>Many patio doors are not perfectly square, especially in older homes, which throws off a flat tape measure reading.</li>
  <li>Door handles, locks, and trim can get in the way of where the headrail needs to sit.</li>
  <li>Floor to ceiling coverage requires an accurate height reading taken at several points along the opening.</li>
</ul>
<h2 id="what-mistakes-do-homeowners-commonly-make-on-their-own">What Mistakes Do Homeowners Commonly Make on Their Own?</h2>
<p><strong>A homeowner measuring without training usually runs into the same handful of problems.</strong></p>
<ul class="bp-list">
  <li>Measuring only once instead of checking width and height in three places.</li>
  <li>Not accounting for the vane or slat overlap needed for full light blockage.</li>
  <li>Ordering an inside mount when the frame depth is too shallow to support it.</li>
  <li>Missing clearance needed for the door handle or the track the door slides on.</li>
  <li>Rounding a measurement up or down instead of recording the exact number.</li>
</ul>
%IMAGE2%
<h2 id="what-does-a-professional-measurement-add-that-a-homeowner-me">What Does a Professional Measurement Add That a Homeowner Measurement Does Not?</h2>
<p><strong>A trained measure technician is checking for more than a single width and height number.</strong></p>
<ul class="bp-list">
  <li>Confirms whether an inside mount or outside mount is actually possible given the frame depth.</li>
  <li>Checks the opening in multiple spots to catch any out of square condition.</li>
  <li>Accounts for hardware clearance around the handle, lock, and sliding track.</li>
  <li>Matches the headrail and vane choice to how the patio door actually opens and closes.</li>
  <li>Documents the exact order so the product that arrives matches the opening the first time.</li>
</ul>
<p>Our <a href="%BASE%/window-treatments/window-treatment-installer/blind-installer/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">blind installers</a> measure every patio door in person before placing an order, which is the step that prevents a vertical blind from arriving too short, too narrow, or unable to mount the way the homeowner expected. <a href="%BASE%/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Creative Blinds and Drapes</a> builds this measurement visit into every patio door quote at no extra cost.</p>
<p>A patio door is one of the few openings in a home where a do it yourself measurement carries real financial risk, since a reorder on a wide vertical blind order is rarely cheap. Homeowners who want to compare the two approaches themselves can read our <a href="%BASE%/blog/is-it-easy-to-install-blinds-yourself/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">guide on whether it is easy to install blinds yourself</a> before deciding. The safer and generally faster path is to <a href="%BASE%/contact/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">schedule a free in-home consultation</a> and let a technician take the measurement the first time.</p>
